cdjot-specific tests: bugs found via corpus comparison against JS djot,
plus regression tests for cdjot bugs and performance fixes. Cases that
upstream has since adopted are covered by the upstream test files.

Inline attributes on links: {key=val}, {.class}, {#id} after
[text](url) should attach to the <a> element.

```
[a](b){rel=me}
.
<p><a href="b" rel="me">a</a></p>
```

```
[a](b){rel="me"}
.
<p><a href="b" rel="me">a</a></p>
```

```
[a](b){.cls #id}
.
<p><a href="b" class="cls" id="id">a</a></p>
```

Attributes are emitted in declaration order, and a repeated name keeps
its original position while taking the later value. Only classes
accumulate; a second id replaces the first rather than joining it.

```
{#i1 .c key=a #i2}
para
.
<p id="i2" class="c" key="a">para</p>
```

Inline attributes on strong should attach to the element,
not wrap in a <span>.

```
*strong*{.a}
.
<p><strong class="a">strong</strong></p>
```

Inline attributes on sub/superscript:

```
~sub~{.a}
.
<p><sub class="a">sub</sub></p>
```

```
^sup^{.a}
.
<p><sup class="a">sup</sup></p>
```

Inline attributes on insert/delete:

```
{+ins+}{.a}
.
<p><ins class="a">ins</ins></p>
```

```
{-del-}{.a}
.
<p><del class="a">del</del></p>
```

Inline attributes on code spans, with a quoted value:

```
`code`{key="value"}
.
<p><code key="value">code</code></p>
```

Inline attributes on autolinks, with id and class:

```
<https://example.com>{#id .cls}
.
<p><a href="https://example.com" id="id" class="cls">https://example.com</a></p>
```

Combined inline attributes with id, class, and key=val:

```
_emph_{#id .cls key=val}
.
<p><em id="id" class="cls" key="val">emph</em></p>
```

An attribute block ahead of a reference definition applies to links
that use it — all of it, id and class included, not just key=value
pairs.

```
{#i .c key=v}
[foo]: http://x

[foo][]
.
<p><a href="http://x" id="i" class="c" key="v">foo</a></p>
```

A footnote reference inside a list item must remain valid after the
list ends, because list items are processed in a temporary recursion
buffer that is freed when the list returns. A later reference to the
same undefined label must still resolve to the same footnote.

```
- [^a]

then [^a]
.
<ul>
<li>
<a id="fnref1" href="#fn1" role="doc-noteref"><sup>1</sup></a>
</li>
</ul>
<p>then <a href="#fn1" role="doc-noteref"><sup>1</sup></a></p>
<section role="doc-endnotes">
<hr>
<ol>
<li id="fn1">
<p><a href="#fnref1" role="doc-backlink">↩︎</a></p>
</li>
</ol>
</section>
```

A reference definition with no URL produces a link with an empty href.
Internally the empty URL must not be passed as a NULL source to memcpy.

```
[link]:

[link][]
.
<p><a href="">link</a></p>
```

Identifier and class names accept only ASCII alphanumerics, `_`, `:`,
and `-` (matching the spec's rule for unquoted key=value values, and
matching djot.js). Any other character invalidates the entire
attribute spec, which then renders as literal text. This prevents
characters like `"` from being smuggled into HTML attribute contexts.
(attributes.test covers the `<` case.)

```
{.x"y}
foo
.
<p>{.x“y}
foo</p>
```

Quoted values are not restricted that way, so they are the one place
`<` and `&` can reach an attribute value. They must be HTML-escaped on
output so they can't break out of the attribute.

```
{k1="a<b" k2="c&d"}
para
.
<p k1="a&lt;b" k2="c&amp;d">para</p>
```

Inline attribute syntax with invalid chars renders the braces as
literal trailing text rather than silently consuming them: trailing
attrs on links, images, autolinks, and reference links are not
consumed. (For spans, the brackets go literal too — see spans.test.)

```
[link](url){.x"y}
.
<p><a href="url">link</a>{.x“y}</p>
```

Smart double quotes: djot.js puts no opener restriction on `"` -- any
`"` may open -- and renders every unmatched one as a left quote, so an
intra-word quote opens rather than closes. cdjot used to choose by
looking at the neighbouring characters, which made this a right quote.

```
x"y
.
<p>x“y</p>
```

Quotes pair left to right, and a `"` following a completed pair opens
a new one rather than closing the old:

```
a"b"c"d
.
<p>a“b”c“d</p>
```

Adjacent delimiters would enclose nothing, so they do not pair. Both
fall back to the unmatched glyph, which is a left quote for `"` and a
right quote for `'`:

```
""
.
<p>““</p>
```

```
''
.
<p>’’</p>
```

Regression: a run of `^` or `_` with no possible closer used to be
O(N^3) — each call to dosurround re-scanned the whole run via an
all-delim walk. Locks the literal-emit fall-through.

```
^^^^^^^^^^
.
<p>^^^^^^^^^^</p>
```

```
__________
.
<p>__________</p>
```

```
___xyz
.
<p>___xyz</p>
```

Regression: consecutive indented `[label]:` lines used to make prescan
re-walk continuations on each iteration (O(N^2)) and merged sibling
refdefs into one URL. Each line should register its own ref.

```
  [a]: http://aa
  [b]: http://bb
[link][b]
.
<p><a href="http://bb">link</a></p>
```

Regression: a paragraph full of `[^foo` (unmatched footnote refs) used
to make dolink and dosurround walk to e on every `[`, O(N^2). The
short-circuit cache makes this linear; output is unchanged.

```
[^x
[^y
.
<p>[<sup>x
[</sup>y</p>
```

Regression: a long run of `{` with no closing `}` used to make
doparagraph's brace pre-process re-scan to e on every `{` — O(N^2).
The no_close_after cache makes this linear; output is unchanged.

```
{{{{{{{{{{
.
<p>{{{{{{{{{{</p>
```

Regression: many `{X` (X non-alpha, no closing `}`) used to make
doreplace re-scan to the next `\n` on every `{` — O(N^2). The
nc_b/nc_eol cache makes this linear; output is unchanged.

```
{1 {1 {1 {1 {1 {1 {1
.
<p>{1 {1 {1 {1 {1 {1 {1</p>
```

Regression: a long whitespace run with no following `\` used to make
doreplace's hard-break check forward-walk on every space — O(N^2).
The b[-1]-is-ws skip makes this linear; output is unchanged.

```
a                                                  b
.
<p>a                                                  b</p>
```

Regression: a footnote whose content has many blank lines used to make
emit_endnotes re-scan via skip_blanks per blank line — O(N^2). Output
shape is unchanged.

```
[^a]

[^a]: line1


  line2
.
<p><a id="fnref1" href="#fn1" role="doc-noteref"><sup>1</sup></a></p>
<section role="doc-endnotes">
<hr>
<ol>
<li id="fn1">
<p>line1</p>
<p>line2<a href="#fnref1" role="doc-backlink">↩︎</a></p>
</li>
</ol>
</section>
```

URL safety: a literal `<` in an image destination must be HTML-escaped
in the emitted src so it can't break out of the attribute. Found via
fuzz/proptest attrsafe on AFL-mutated URLs. (The link equivalents are
in links_and_images.test.)

```
![alt](/img/<svg)
.
<p><img alt="alt" src="/img/&lt;svg"></p>
```

A bare `key` or an empty `key=` is not valid attribute syntax: the spec
allows only `.class`, `#id`, `key=value`, `key="value"` and `%comment%`.
An unparseable `{...}` makes the whole construct literal text, including
the surrounding brackets (jgm/djot#399), so no attribute in the run is
applied and a failed block attribute line starts a paragraph.

```
[x]{key}

[y]{key=}

{.a key}
# not a heading
.
<p>[x]{key}</p>
<p>[y]{key=}</p>
<p>{.a key}
# not a heading</p>
```

Headings used to emit only id and class, silently dropping any other
key=value attribute. All attributes are emitted, in declaration order.
The id is the exception: it goes on the enclosing <section>, and only
when a heading is inside a container (so no section is opened) does it
appear on the tag itself -- prepended when auto-generated, in place when
written explicitly.

```
{a=b .c}
# abc

:::
{a=b #x}
## sub
:::
.
<section id="abc">
<h1 a="b" class="c">abc</h1>
<div>
<h2 a="b" id="x">sub</h2>
</div>
</section>
```

That rule holds whether the id is auto-generated or written out. djot.js
agrees on the auto-generated case above, but moves the whole attribute
set onto the <section> when the id is explicit, leaving the heading bare
-- so this one case diverges. djot-php matches cdjot. Which behaviour is
right is open upstream as jgm/djot.js#144.

```
{a=b .c #x}
# abc
.
<section id="x">
<h1 a="b" class="c">abc</h1>
</section>
```
